@h1 The draft workflow h1@

Here is the story that sells Wit to a working writer: your manuscript is plain
text in a git repository, you compile a reader-ready PDF in seconds, and every
draft is versioned and diff-able like code. No binary document format, no lost
track changes, no "final_final_v3.docx". Just text you own and outputs you
generate on demand.

@h2 Your manuscript is text h2@

A manuscript is one or more @code .wit code@ files in a folder under git. You
edit in whatever editor you like, and git tracks every change — line by line, in
plain language you can actually read in a diff.

@h2 Compile a reader copy h2@

When you want something to send, build a PDF:

@@@pre
wit build manuscript.wit -o draft.pdf
pre@@@

That renders your source through a headless browser into a paginated,
print-ready PDF. Send it, and keep writing — the source never leaves your repo.

@h2 Every draft is diff-able h2@

Because the source is text, a change between drafts is a readable diff — you see
exactly which sentences moved, not an opaque blob of bytes. Commit your drafts as
you go, and the history of your thinking is right there:

@@@pre
git add manuscript.wit
git commit -m "Tighten the opening; cut the second epigraph"
pre@@@

@h2 Versioned drafts h2@

Tag or branch a draft the way you would any code. Compare last week's version to
today's with an ordinary diff; keep a branch for an experimental restructure you
are not sure about. The full apparatus of version control is available to your
prose, for free.

@h2 Normalise before committing h2@

Wit ships a formatter that re-indents a file to match its real structure, so your
source stays tidy and your diffs stay clean. It writes in place with a flag:

@@@pre
wit fmt manuscript.wit -w
pre@@@

It reports whether it reformatted or left the file unchanged, and it requires the
file to parse first — so a formatter run doubles as a quick sanity check that the
document is well formed.

@aside(class note) 💡 One honest limit: there is no built-in watch mode — no
command that recompiles automatically as you type. The "instant recompile" story
is simply that the build is fast, so you re-run it. If you want automatic rebuilds,
wrap the build in a shell loop or a file-watcher tool such as one that reruns a
command when a file changes. aside@

@h2 One more honest limit: PDF needs a browser h2@

PDF output is rendered through a local copy of Chrome or Chromium (or one you
point to with an environment variable). If no browser is found, the build stops
and tells you so, with guidance to render to HTML and convert that instead. HTML
and Markdown output have no such dependency.

@h2 The shape of a working setup h2@

@ul
@li A folder under git holding your @code .wit code@ source. li@
@li A build command you run to produce a PDF (or HTML, or Markdown) to share. li@
@li A formatter run before you commit, to keep diffs clean. li@
@li Tags or branches for drafts you want to compare. li@
ul@

